#open-question(title: [Do the Non-Anticoagulant Trial Arms Justify a Study in ME/CFS?])[
The same trial that refuted the anticoagulant rationale produced two nominally positive signals in its other arms. Whether those signals justify a trial in ME/CFS is unresolved, and the case for one is weaker than the headline numbers suggest.

In 778 patients with the post-COVID-19 condition, colchicine reduced fatigue by 1.49 points (95% confidence interval $-2.92$ to $-0.06$, $p = 0.041$) and famotidine plus loratadine by 1.48 points ($-2.88$ to $-0.08$, $p = 0.038$), against a rivaroxaban arm that showed nothing @StimulateICP2026rivaroxaban. Four features limit what those differences mean.

*Size.* The Fatigue Assessment Scale runs from 10 to 50, and the trial defined a change above 10% — roughly 4 points — as clinically meaningful. A 1.5-point difference is well below that threshold, so the effects are statistically detectable and clinically small.

*Durability.* No group differed at 24 weeks, 12 weeks after the drugs stopped. A treatment that works only while being taken, by an amount below the clinically meaningful threshold, is a weak candidate.

*Blinding.* The trial was open-label. Participants knew whether they were receiving a drug, and the no-drug arm knew it was receiving usual care alone, which is a direct route to expectancy effects on a self-reported fatigue endpoint.

*Multiplicity.* Three active arms were compared against one control. Two nominally significant results at $p < 0.05$ without a reported adjustment is compatible with chance, and the abstract does not report one.

Two practical obstacles sit on top of these. Colchicine is a CYP3A4 and P-glycoprotein substrate, and co-administration with strong inhibitors such as clarithromycin or azole antifungals causes toxicity, while concomitant statin use raises myopathy risk. Famotidine reduces gastric acidity and alters the absorption of drugs that require it; the trade-offs between the two H2 blockers are treated in Section @sec:h2-comparison. Neither drug has been studied for fatigue in ME/CFS.

*Falsifiable prediction:* a blinded, placebo-controlled trial in ME/CFS with a prespecified clinically meaningful difference of at least 4 Fatigue Assessment Scale points, sustained at 24 weeks, would establish a signal. An effect that disappears under blinding, or that does not reach 4 points in an adequately powered trial, would fail to support the current reading.

*Consequence:* Patients should not take colchicine or famotidine plus loratadine for ME/CFS fatigue on the basis of this trial. The signal is small, brief, and unblinded, and the interaction and monitoring burden is real. (Origin: brainstorm.)
] <oq:non-anticoagulant-arms-signal>
